::PC_U03_L07_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L07_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.12
Question:
A Ferris wheel has maximum height 90 and minimum height 30. What is the amplitude?
{
=A. 30#Correct. Amplitude is half the distance between maximum and minimum: (90-30)/2=30.
~B. 60#This is the maximum, not amplitude.
~C. 90#This adds max and min instead of finding half the range.
~D. 120#
}
::PC_U03_L07_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L07_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.12
Question:
A periodic model repeats every 24 hours. What is the period?
{
~A. 12 hours#This is half the cycle.
=B. 24 hours#Correct. The period is the time for one complete cycle.
~C. 48 hours#This does not match the stated repeating interval.
~D. 2 hours#
}
::PC_U03_L07_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L07_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.14
Question:
Why might context restrict a trigonometric model domain?
{
~A. Because sine is never defined#Sine is defined for all real inputs.
~B. Because all trig graphs are finite only#Some trig functions have restrictions, but context is the issue here.
=C. Because the situation may only be observed for a certain time#Correct. A model can be mathematically defined beyond the observed context.
~D. Because radians cannot be graphed#
}
::PC_U03_L07_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L07_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.12
Question:
Which features are needed for a sine or cosine model?
{
~A. Only zeros#Zeros alone are not enough.
~B. Only denominator restrictions#Denominator restrictions are not the main sinusoidal features.
~C. Only polynomial degree#Polynomial degree is not a trigonometric graph feature.
=D. Amplitude, period, midline, and shift#Correct. These features describe sinusoidal models.
}
::PC_U03_L07_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L07_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.14
Question:
A bounded height that rises and falls smoothly is usually better modeled by which function family?
{
=A. Sine or cosine#Correct. Sine and cosine model smooth bounded periodic behavior.
~B. Rational only#Linear functions do not repeat.
~C. Linear only#Tangent is periodic but unbounded with asymptotes.
~D. Tangent only#
}